AB  - Existing theoretical models for predicting sulfate-induced expansion are inadequate for capturing the complex interactions arising from moisture cycling and mechanical loading. In this study, acid-soluble and water-soluble sulfate ion distributions in concrete were experimentally measured, and a chemical attack proportion coefficient was introduced to quantify the relative contributions of chemical and physical sulfate attacks to concrete deterioration. A multi-field coupled model was developed, incorporating moisture transport, calcium leaching, crystalline transitions, and the effects of external loading. The model was validated under representative conditions, including sulfate attack combined with wet-dry cycles, static loading, and fatigue loading. The effects of dry-to-wet ratio, chemical reaction coefficient, stress level, and loading frequency were systematically analyzed. The proposed framework provides a robust basis for durability assessment and service life prediction of concrete structures exposed to sulfate-rich environments.
