AB  - This study examined position-specific physical and technical demands in elite men's team handball during the EHF EURO 2022, focusing on players with greatest on-court time to enhance ecological validity. Tracking data from 780 match observations (six outfield players with the highest playing time, per team, per match, in 65 matches) were analysed using a local positioning system (Kinexon LPS). Positional differences were identified across locomotor, acceleration, jumps, impacts, and technical metrics. Wings played the most minutes (similar to 44 min), covered the greatest total distance (LW: 3620 +/- 107 m; RW: 3639 +/- 108 m), and achieved the highest values in running, high-speed running, and sprinting. Backcourt players recorded the most changes of direction (0.3-0.4/min), jumps (0.46-0.50/min), and ball-related actions. Centre backs covered the most distance per minute (88.9 +/- 1.1 m/min), reached the highest acceleration load (10.57 +/- 0.15 AU/min), and made the most passes (4.08 +/- 0.14/min), underpinning their central role in both offence and defence. Pivots showed the lowest movement demands (75.6 +/- 1.0 m/min), but the highest ball recoveries (0.19 +/- 0.01/min), and high number of impacts (0.35 +/- 0.02/min), reflecting frequent involvement in physical duels. These findings highlight differentiated positional profiles based on players with the highest playing time, providing detailed benchmarks for tailoring position-specific conditioning, tactical preparation, and individualised load management in elite team handball.
