AB  - This study compared the physical demands between domestic and international football matches and examined differences across playing positions. Participants were professional soccer players from the Kosovo Super League (domestic) and UEFA Conference League (international). Physical performance variables included total distance, distance per minute, high-speed running (HSR) distance, sprint distance, number of sprints, accelerations, and decelerations. Data were analyzed using paired t-tests, Wilcoxon tests, and one-way ANOVA (p < 0.05). Players covered significantly greater total distance in international matches (10,948.5 +/- 1,299.9 m) than in domestic ones (10,325.9 +/- 782.5 m; t = -3.33, p = 0.002, d = 0.49). Distance per minute was also higher internationally (115.2 +/- 15.1 vs. 109.2 +/- 8.6 m/min; p = 0.013, d = 0.38), indicating increased match workload. No significant differences were found in HSR, sprint distance, sprints, accelerations, and decelerations (p > 0.05). Position-wise, midfielders displayed the highest workload in total distance (10,829.23 +/- 1,267.65 m) and accelerations (46.23 +/- 16.39), followed by forwards, while defenders showed the lowest values. Significant effects of position were found for HSR distance (p = 0.048) and accelerations (p = 0.012), with midfielders and forwards performing more high-intensity efforts. Sprinting and deceleration activities were not statistically significant in attacking roles. In conclusion, international matches impose greater overall locomotor volume, while positional analysis highlights midfielders and forwards as the most active roles in high-speed and acceleration efforts, reflecting their tactical responsibilities.
