KW  - BEHAVIOR
KW  - DECK
KW  - STRENGTH
KW  - GIRDERS
AB  - Cracking of shoulder beams in Concrete Filled Steel Tubular (CFST) latticed columns under cyclic crane loads is a widespread issue in heavy industrial plants worldwide, posing substantial threats to operational safety. However, the underlying cracking mechanism remains poorly understood, primarily due to the complex interplay of Welding Residual Stresses (WRS) and long-term localized cyclic loading. To address this, fatigue performance tests were conducted on four single-web shoulder beam specimens. A device based on the stress-magnetic coupling relationship was employed to monitor WRS and its relaxation in the ortho-welded zone of the shortspan section. Results indicate that the superposition of high-amplitude tensile WRS and cyclic compressive stresses from external loading transforms the normal stress state at the crack plane from compressivecompressive cycling (stress ratio R > 10) to tensile-tensile cycling (0 < R < 1). Consequently, the mean stress increases from between -40 and - 80 MPa to between 150 and 220 MPa. This shift explains the fundamental mechanism of compressive cracking at horizontal welds within the ortho-welded zone. Moreover, omitting chamfering elevates WRS levels, leading to higher mean stress and reducing fatigue life by up to 11.5%. The proposed strengthening method-welding reinforcing plates-extends the fatigue life of ortho-welded zone welds by over 200% by reducing cyclic stress amplitude. Furthermore, a fatigue life prediction model accounting for WRS relaxation was developed. When combined with the hot spot stress-based FAT 90 S-N curve, it provides reliable predictions for the fatigue life of ortho-welded details in the short-span region of the shoulder beam.
